[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m c_src/src/lib.c:12:                !((((h1[2]) & 0xF0) == 0) ^ (((h2[2]) & 0xF0) == 0));
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m c_src/src/lib.c:13:     }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Findings that shape this table:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - There are **no** error macros, no `errno`, no error enum, no `assert`, no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `abort`, no `NULL` check, no allocation, and no `#ifdef` in the C source.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - There are **no enum parameters** anywhere in the public API (`lib.h` declares
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   one function taking two `const uint8_t *`), so "out-of-range enum value across
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the FFI boundary" has no instance in this library. The analogous class of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   input — a byte value with no meaningful interpretation, e.g. a reserved
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   bitrate/sampling-rate field — *does* exist and is rows 4–6 below.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - The function's only failure signal is its **return value: `0` = rejected,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `1` = accepted** (`int`, produced by C's `&&`/`!` operators, which yield
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   exactly `0` or `1`). "Same error code" in Phase C therefore means "both sides
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   return the identical `int`".
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Each term of the two `&&` chains is a distinct rejection branch, and C's `&&`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   **short-circuits**: a rejection at term *n* means terms *n+1…* are never
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   evaluated, so `h1` is never dereferenced when `hdr_valid(h2)` is false. That
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   short-circuit is itself an observable behaviour (row 11) and is tested.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Error-surface table
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rows 1–5 are the rejection branches inside `hdr_valid`, reached via
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `hdr_compare`'s first term with `h = h2`. Rows 6–9 are `hdr_compare`'s own
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m terms. Rows 10–13 are the generic FFI boundary conditions.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | # | function | trigger (the exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|----------|----------------------------------------------|-------------------|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 1 | `hdr_valid` (via `hdr_compare`, `h=h2`) | `h2[0] != 0xff` — first sync byte wrong. Any of the 255 non-`0xff` values, with `h2[1]`/`h2[2]`/`h1` otherwise fully valid & matching | returns `0` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 2 | `hdr_valid` (via `hdr_compare`) | `(h2[1] & 0xF0) != 0xf0` **and** `(h2[1] & 0xFE) != 0xe2` — second sync byte in neither accepted class. The 238 values outside `{0xe2,0xe3} ∪ [0xf0,0xff]` | returns `0` |
